---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import PhotosynthesisSim from '../../simulators/photosynthesis/PhotosynthesisSim.astro';
import { photosynthesisQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/photosynthesis';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is photosynthesis?' },
  { id: 'where', label: 'Where it happens' },
  { id: 'stages', label: 'The two stages' },
  { id: 'limiting', label: 'Limiting factors' },
  { id: 'experiment', label: 'The pondweed experiment' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Equations' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 8: Photosynthesis. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-introduction' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 8.2: The Light-Dependent Reaction of Photosynthesis. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reaction-of-photosynthesis' },
  { text: 'Blackman, F. F. (1905). Optima and limiting factors. Annals of Botany, 19(74), 281–295.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="photosynthesis"
  description="Interactive photosynthesis simulator: move a lamp, change carbon dioxide, temperature and light colour, count oxygen bubbles from pondweed and discover limiting factors."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={photosynthesisQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Almost every food chain on Earth begins with a plant capturing sunlight. Through photosynthesis, plants turn light,
      water and carbon dioxide into sugar — and release the oxygen we breathe. Recreate a classic school experiment:
      light a piece of pondweed, count its oxygen bubbles, and find out what limits how fast it can work.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <PhotosynthesisSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong>. One second equals one minute; oxygen bubbles rise from the pondweed and are counted.</li>
      <li><strong>Drag the lamp</strong> (or use the slider) to change its distance. Watch the bubble rate and the relative light intensity.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>carbon dioxide</strong>, <strong>temperature</strong> and <strong>light colour</strong> one at a time.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Record</strong> at several distances. Your points appear on the graph alongside the model’s prediction (dashed).</li>
      <li>Read the <strong>limiting factor</strong> readout to see what is holding the plant back. <strong>Reset</strong> clears your results.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is photosynthesis?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Photosynthesis</strong> is the process by which plants, algae and some bacteria use light energy to make
    glucose from carbon dioxide and water. The energy stored in glucose powers the plant’s growth, and glucose is also
    turned into starch for storage and cellulose for cell walls. Oxygen is released as a by-product. Animals, including
    humans, depend on both the food and the oxygen that photosynthesis produces.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where it happens</h2>
  <p>
    Photosynthesis takes place in <strong>chloroplasts</strong>, mainly in the palisade cells of leaves. Chloroplasts
    contain the green pigment <strong>chlorophyll</strong>, which absorbs red and blue light strongly and reflects green.
    Carbon dioxide enters the leaf through stomata, and water arrives from the roots through the xylem.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stages">The two stages</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Light-dependent reactions</strong> (in the thylakoid membranes): light energy splits water molecules, releasing oxygen and producing the energy carriers ATP and NADPH.</li>
    <li><strong>The Calvin cycle</strong> (in the stroma): the enzyme RuBisCO fixes carbon dioxide, and ATP and NADPH are used to build sugar.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="limiting">Limiting factors</h2>
  <p>
    The rate of photosynthesis depends on several factors. At any moment, the factor in shortest supply sets the
    maximum rate — this is the <strong>principle of limiting factors</strong>, described by Frederick Blackman in 1905.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light intensity:</strong> more light speeds up the light-dependent reactions, until something else limits the rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Carbon dioxide concentration:</strong> more CO₂ speeds up the Calvin cycle, up to a point.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> warmth speeds up the enzymes, but above about 35–40 °C they begin to denature and the rate falls rapidly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On a graph of rate against light intensity, the curve rises and then flattens into a plateau. The height of the
    plateau is set by the next limiting factor — try raising CO₂ and recording again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="experiment">The pondweed experiment</h2>
  <p>
    Aquatic plants such as <em>Elodea</em> release oxygen as visible bubbles. Placing a lamp at different distances
    and counting bubbles per minute gives a measure of the rate. Sodium hydrogen carbonate added to the water provides
    carbon dioxide. To keep the test fair, a glass of water (or an LED lamp) can be used to stop the lamp from heating the
    water.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Equations</h2>
  <code class="formula">carbon dioxide + water  —(light, chlorophyll)→  glucose + oxygen</code>
  <code class="formula">6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂</code>
  <code class="formula">Light intensity ∝ 1 / distance²</code>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Chlorophyll</dt>
    <dd>The green pigment in chloroplasts that absorbs light energy.</dd>
    <dt>Chloroplast</dt>
    <dd>The organelle where photosynthesis takes place.</dd>
    <dt>Limiting factor</dt>
    <dd>The condition in shortest supply, which restricts the rate of a process.</dd>
    <dt>Inverse-square law</dt>
    <dd>The intensity of light from a point source is inversely proportional to the square of the distance.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Greenhouses</strong> boost yields by adding artificial light, heating and extra carbon dioxide.</li>
    <li><strong>Food security:</strong> scientists are engineering crops with more efficient photosynthesis.</li>
    <li><strong>Climate:</strong> photosynthesis by plants and ocean phytoplankton removes vast amounts of CO₂ from the atmosphere each year.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxygen:</strong> almost all the oxygen in the atmosphere was produced by photosynthesis.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Plants get their food from the soil.”</strong> Most of a plant’s mass comes from carbon dioxide in the air.</li>
    <li><strong>“Plants photosynthesise but don’t respire.”</strong> Plants respire all the time; in bright light, photosynthesis is simply faster.</li>
    <li><strong>“More light always means more photosynthesis.”</strong> Only until another factor becomes limiting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Photosynthesis uses light to turn CO₂ and water into glucose and oxygen.</li>
    <li>Light intensity falls with the square of the distance from the lamp.</li>
    <li>The rate is set by the limiting factor: light, CO₂ or temperature.</li>
    <li>High temperatures denature enzymes and stop photosynthesis.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: the bubble rate is a simplified model; real bubbles vary in size and some oxygen dissolves in the water.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
